course: dictionary
lesson: 1558
title: One word, many channels
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have several meanings, and the words around it tell you which one is meant. Read the sentence first. «The tide comes in across flat sand faster than you can walk and fills channels behind you first.» Here a channel is a narrow watery passage in the sand, not a TV station.
explain: «The Channel Tunnel is really three tunnels, two for trains and one service tunnel between them.» The capital C is a clue: Channel is a place name, the sea between England and France. Capital letters often show that a word is a name.
explain: Channel can also be a verb. To channel energy means to direct it to one place or purpose, such as sending your energy into sport. Put your meaning into the sentence and check: does it still make sense? If it does, you have found the right meaning.
fact: A channel is a TV or radio station, or a stream of online videos, like a cartoon channel.
fact: A channel is a narrow stretch of sea between two pieces of land, such as the English Channel.
fact: A channel is a long narrow passage that water flows along, like a ditch or a deep part of a river where ships can sail.
fact: A channel is also a way of communicating or sending information, as in «through official channels».
fact: To channel something means to direct it to one place or purpose, such as water, energy or money.
exercise: «The tide comes in across flat sand faster than you can walk and fills channels behind you first.» In this sentence, what is a channel? | A TV station | A way of sending messages | A type of boat | *A narrow watery passage in the sand
exercise: The English Channel separates which two countries? | Spain and Portugal | *England and France | Wales and Scotland
exercise: Mia says, «I want to channel my energy into football.» What does channel mean here? | *To direct energy to one purpose | To dig a ditch | To change the TV | To send a letter
test: Dad says, «Change the channel, the film is over.» What does channel mean? | *A TV station | A kind of boat | A narrow stretch of sea | A long, wide road
test: The school sent the news «through official channels.» What does channels mean here? | *Ways of sending information | Deep ditches for water | Swimming lanes in a pool | Tunnels under the sea
exam: Water flows along a channel that a river has carved over many years. What is a channel in this sentence? | A TV station | A narrow stretch of sea between two countries | A way of sending messages | *A long narrow passage that water flows along
